---
/**
 * Homepage hero (Figma node 770:390). Pinned via CSS `position: sticky` so
 * later sections scroll up and cover it — no JS needed for the pin itself.
 * The nav is part of this section's own content (overlaid, transparent,
 * light text) rather than BaseLayout's default bordered header — see
 * hideHeader on BaseLayout.
 *
 * Background is the paper-design Heatmap shader (@paper-design/shaders),
 * mounted only here — no other page carries a shader hero, so nothing else
 * pays the WebGL cost. The uniform values below are copied from the Paper
 * editor. Speed is intentionally low. ShaderMount halts its rAF loop
 * entirely at speed 0 and pauses itself while the tab is hidden — but not
 * while this section is merely covered, because a sticky element never
 * leaves the viewport. The script below closes that gap: the shader is
 * stopped once the page has scrolled past the hero, rendered at CSS
 * resolution rather than the library's 2x minimum, and not mounted at all
 * under prefers-reduced-motion or Save-Data, where the still is the same
 * image anyway.
 *
 * Three assets in public/hero/, all baked by tools/bake-hero-assets.html —
 * regenerate all three together whenever the shape changes:
 *
 * - hero-heatmap-processed.webp the shape run through toProcessedHeatmap()
 * - hero-still{,-mobile}.webp   frame 0 of the finished shader, gradient baked in
 *
 * The shape they are all derived from is the authored 1:1 source at
 * src/assets/hero/hero-heatmap-shape.webp. It is deliberately *not* in
 * public/: nothing fetches it at runtime, so publishing it only added weight
 * to dist/. The bake page reads it through Vite's dev server instead.
 *
 * toProcessedHeatmap() packs three blur passes of the shape into one RGB
 * texture, and it used to run in the browser on every page load: ~810ms of
 * blocking main-thread work on an idle desktop, over 3s on a page that is
 * still loading, and multiples of that on a phone. The result depends only
 * on the source file, so it is baked once instead — the runtime now just
 * loads an image (~40ms). See tools/bake-hero-assets.html for the numbers.
 *
 * The stills close the remaining gap. They are frame 0 of the shader with
 * this section's own CSS gradient composited underneath, so the very first
 * paint is already the full effect rather than a bare gradient; the canvas
 * cross-fades over the still once WebGL is up, and both ends of that fade
 * are the same image.
 *
 * They are baked at a typical laptop (16:10) and a typical phone (~390x844)
 * rather than square, because the virtual frame (src/lib/heatmapFrame.ts,
 * §69) puts the edge glow on the viewport's own edges: a still only carries
 * it at the aspect it was baked at. `background-size: cover` crops them to
 * any other ratio, so a 16:9 or ultrawide hero opens with a little of the
 * top/bottom glow trimmed for the 200ms before the canvas takes over. Baking
 * at the real aspect also means the gradient half is laid out in the same
 * box percentages as the live one, which the square stills couldn't do.
 *
 * The mobile still bakes in MOBILE_OFFSET_X, so its media query below has to
 * stay in sync with MOBILE_QUERY in the script — which gates on aspect ratio
 * as well as width, for the reason set out where it's declared.
 */
import { navItems, isNavActive } from '../lib/nav'
import Heading from './Heading.astro'
import AnimatedLogo from './AnimatedLogo.astro'
import StickyHero from './StickyHero.astro'
---

{
  /* Sizing against iOS Safari's toolbar, in CSS alone.

     iOS 26 Safari floats its bottom toolbar over the page instead of
     shrinking it, so the hero has two heights to respect:

     - 100lvh, the large viewport (toolbar out of the way), is the box. The
       background bleeds behind the toolbar instead of leaving a strip of
       the next (white) section showing round it.
     - 100svh, the small viewport (toolbar fully shown), is what is
       guaranteed visible. Their difference, --hero-chrome, is added to the
       bottom padding so the headline and lead stay above the toolbar.

     Both units are static: unlike dvh they don't move while the toolbar
     shows and hides, nor on the first paint before Safari has "settled"
     (the reason a JS measurement used to write these as --hero-vh /
     --hero-chrome on every resize). Nor do they react to an open keyboard
     or to desktop pinch-zoom, which is what the old script needed a
     pointer-type split and a 25% clamp to tell apart from a toolbar.
     --hero-chrome is registered as a <length> (see <style>) so its computed
     value is plain px, which the runway script reads back.

     There is no min-height floor any more. It was min(45rem, 100lvh) — never
     a bare 45rem, which parked the bottom-anchored copy past the fold on
     short screens — and with the box now exactly 100lvh it was a no-op.
     Layout and scroll behaviour live in StickyHero. */
}
<StickyHero
  unit="lvh"
  ink="light"
  style="--hero-chrome: calc(100lvh - 100svh)"
  bgStyle="background: radial-gradient(55% 60% at 28% 38%, #4A40F2 0%, transparent 62%), radial-gradient(50% 55% at 78% 62%, #2E0EB2 0%, transparent 68%), linear-gradient(to right, #4A40F2, #0E004D);"
  copyClass="mb-[calc(var(--space-gutter)_+_var(--hero-chrome,0px))]"
  dimMax={0.6}
  drift={0.06}
>
  <Fragment slot="background">
  {
    /* The section's own CSS gradient (above) stays the bottom layer: the
       shader's colorBack is transparent, so it composites over this rather
       than replacing it. #hero-still sits between the two — see the docblock.

       Preloaded because a CSS background-image isn't visible to the preload
       scanner: it isn't requested until the element has been laid out, which
       is exactly the delay the still exists to remove. The two `media` attrs
       partition the viewport so only one of them is ever fetched. */
  }
  <link
    rel="preload"
    as="image"
    href="/hero/hero-still.webp"
    media="not all and (max-width: 1023px) and (max-aspect-ratio: 3/5)"
  />
  <link
    rel="preload"
    as="image"
    href="/hero/hero-still-mobile.webp"
    media="(max-width: 1023px) and (max-aspect-ratio: 3/5)"
  />
  <div
    id="hero-still"
    aria-hidden="true"
    class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 size-full bg-cover bg-center transition-opacity duration-200 ease-out"
  >
  </div>
  <div
    id="hero-shader"
    aria-hidden="true"
    class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 size-full opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 ease-out"
  >
  </div>

  </Fragment>

  {
    /*
      The logo lives here, in flow, at every width — it scrolls away with
      this sticky section and gets covered by later content exactly as it
      always did. Only the Menu trigger is pinned now (<FixedNav />, mobile
      only, rendered by index.astro alongside this component): the logo
      doesn't reappear on scroll-up the way the trigger does, by design —
      see docs/parked-decisions.md. Nav links stay desktop-only, unchanged.
    */
  }
  {
    /* No z-index on this nav: it would make it a stacking context, and
       AnimatedLogo's screen blend would composite against transparency
       instead of the shader. DOM order already paints it above the
       background layers; the z-20 dim still covers it. The delay holds the
       logo until the h1's line entrance (0.15s + 0.9s + stagger, after
       fonts — motion.ts SPLIT) has landed, so the two never compete. */
  }
  <nav slot="nav" aria-label="Primary" class="relative">
    <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-6">
      <a href="/" class="shrink-0">
        <AnimatedLogo variant="light" delay={1400} />
      </a>
      <ul class="hidden items-center lg:flex lg:gap-[var(--space-nav-gap)]">
        {
          navItems.map((item) => (
            <li>
              <a
                href={item.href}
                aria-current={isNavActive(Astro.url.pathname, item.href) ? 'page' : undefined}
                class:list={[
                  'text-body',
                  isNavActive(Astro.url.pathname, item.href) ? 'text-white' : 'text-azure-50 hover:text-white',
                ]}
              >
                {item.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <Fragment>
    {
      /* No max-width: the headline wraps at the gutter, per brand direction
         that hero titles run the full measure of the page. It previously
         carried a `22ch` / `lg:50vw` cap — ch rather than px, so the wrap
         point stayed proportional as text-display's fluid size grew. That
         reasoning still holds for any heading that DOES want a cap; it just
         no longer applies here, where the container edge is the wrap point.

         The tradeoff this accepts: at very wide viewports the line length
         runs past a comfortable reading measure. That's deliberate for
         display type, where line length is a composition decision — see
         --measure-prose in global.css, which is the token for the cases
         where it isn't. */
    }
    <Heading as="h1" indent={false} class="text-display text-balance text-neutral-50" data-anim="split-text">
      We make tech companies impossible to ignore
    </Heading>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lead text-neutral-50" data-anim="reveal" data-reveal="items">
      We help startups, tech companies and disruptive businesses effectively communicate their brand and their
      people.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  {
    /* Depth cue for the sticky hero: the page slides up over it, and this
       layer darkens it as it goes, so the content reads as a sheet laid on
       top rather than the hero being pushed off. Same move as the footer
       reveal at the other end of the page (BaseLayout's
       footer[data-reveal='on']::after) — a flat neutral-950 layer, not a
       filter, so nothing underneath repaints. Driven by the script below;
       at rest (and without JS) it's fully transparent. */
  }
  <div slot="overlay" data-hero-overlay aria-hidden="true" class="pointer-events-none bg-neutral-950 opacity-0"></div>
</StickyHero>

<style>
  /* Registered so the computed value is resolved px rather than the
     unevaluated calc() string — the runway script reads it back. */
  @property --hero-chrome {
    syntax: '<length>';
    inherits: true;
    initial-value: 0px;
  }

  /* Query has to match MOBILE_QUERY in the script — both halves of it. The
     mobile still has MOBILE_OFFSET_X baked into it, so the two must switch
     together or the cross-fade jumps sideways; and the pan only fits inside
     the texture below 3/5, which is derived where MOBILE_QUERY is declared. */
  #hero-still {
    background-image: url('/hero/hero-still.webp');
  }

  @media (max-width: 1023px) and (max-aspect-ratio: 3/5) {
    #hero-still {
      background-image: url('/hero/hero-still-mobile.webp');
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { onPageLoad, onPageUnload } from '../lib/lifecycle'
  import {
    ShaderMount,
    heatmapFragmentShader,
    getShaderColorFromString,
    ShaderFitOptions,
  } from '@paper-design/shaders'
  import { withViewportFrame } from '../lib/heatmapFrame'

  // The shape asset wants to be authored 1:1. toProcessedHeatmap() (run at
  // bake time now, not here) letterboxes it into a 1000px box, surrounds that
  // with 375px of blur margin on every side, and the fragment shader then
  // un-pads by a *fixed* 1000/1750 on both axes — which is only exact for a
  // square source. The original 16:9 export padded to 1750x1312, where the
  // shape fills just 562/1312 vertically against the 1000/1750 the shader
  // assumes, so ~13% of bare padding showed at each end: the band that
  // appeared once the window grew taller than 16:9.
  //
  // With a square asset the two agree, fillScale() below is a flat 1, and
  // cover fit crops the top and bottom of a tall window while keeping the
  // full width of the frame on screen. It's derived from the processed
  // texture rather than hardcoded so that a non-square export degrades to a
  // zoom-to-fill instead of silently bringing the band back — that costs a
  // tighter crop on the other axis, which is the reason to keep it square.
  // The check still earns its place now that the texture is pre-baked: it's
  // the bake step that could be handed a non-square shape.
  const PROCESS_BOX = 1000 // toProcessedHeatmap's longest-side box
  const PROCESS_PADDING = 375 // blur margin it adds on every side

  // Pre-baked by tools/bake-hero-assets.html — see the docblock at the top.
  const PROCESSED_TEXTURE = '/hero/hero-heatmap-processed.webp'

  function fillScale(image: HTMLImageElement, container: HTMLElement) {
    const textureWidth = image.naturalWidth
    const textureHeight = image.naturalHeight
    // Share of the texture the shader assumes the shape covers, over the
    // share it actually covers, per axis.
    const assumed = PROCESS_BOX / (PROCESS_BOX + 2 * PROCESS_PADDING)
    const deficitX = assumed / ((textureWidth - 2 * PROCESS_PADDING) / textureWidth)
    const deficitY = assumed / ((textureHeight - 2 * PROCESS_PADDING) / textureHeight)
    // Mirrors the vertex shader's own cover box for the image layer: the
    // overflowing axis stays at 1, the other shrinks by the aspect gap.
    const textureAspect = textureWidth / textureHeight
    const aspect = (container.clientWidth || 1) / (container.clientHeight || 1)
    return Math.max(
      Math.min(1, aspect / textureAspect) * deficitX,
      Math.min(1, textureAspect / aspect) * deficitY,
    )
  }

  // Panning toward the left lobe on narrow viewports is art direction, not
  // a fix: filling the height of a phone crops most of the width away, and
  // the gap between the two lobes reads better there than a lobe's middle.
  //
  // The aspect-ratio half of the query is what keeps that pan from running
  // off the end of the texture. `cover` sizes the image box to
  // max(width, height) for a square texture, so the horizontal slack the pan
  // eats into is entirely the amount by which the box is taller than it is
  // wide. The vertex shader maps the container's right edge to
  // `0.5 * width/box - u_offsetX + 0.5` in texture space, so the pan stays
  // inside the texture only while width/box <= 1 - 2*|MOBILE_OFFSET_X|, i.e.
  // 0.6 — a hero taller than 5:3. Past that the last columns sample the
  // texture's clamped edge, which is bare blur margin: it painted as a flat
  // vertical band down the right of the hero, hard-edged at exactly 80% of
  // the width. Every portrait phone is around 0.42-0.56 and unaffected; what
  // hit the band was everything else under 1024px — a tablet in portrait
  // (0.75), a phone in landscape, a half-screen desktop window.
  //
  // Both halves have to stay in sync with the stills' media queries above:
  // the mobile still bakes MOBILE_OFFSET_X in, so it carries the same band
  // and has to stop being served at the same line the pan stops being used.
  const MOBILE_QUERY = '(max-width: 1023px) and (max-aspect-ratio: 3/5)'
  const MOBILE_OFFSET_X = -0.2

  function viewParams(image: HTMLImageElement, container: HTMLElement) {
    return {
      u_scale: fillScale(image, container),
      u_offsetX: mobileQuery?.matches ? MOBILE_OFFSET_X : 0,
    }
  }

  // Copied from the Paper editor, like the uniforms.
  const SPEED = 0.5

  // §69: moves the heatmap's frame glow to the viewport edge on
  // the axis `cover` crops — see src/lib/heatmapFrame.ts. 0 is the stock
  // shader's look, 1 the full virtual frame. If the patch can't find its
  // anchor (a library update), the stock shader runs and this does nothing.
  // In dev, Shift+F toggles it on the live hero for comparison.
  const FRAME_STRENGTH = 1
  const FRAGMENT_SHADER = withViewportFrame(heatmapFragmentShader) ?? heatmapFragmentShader

  // Shader pixels per CSS pixel. ShaderMount defaults to a *minimum* of 2x
  // (and a phone's native 3x), which suits the crisp-edged shaders in its
  // catalogue. The library only exposes a pixel-count ceiling, so the ceiling
  // is derived from the container's CSS area and re-derived as it resizes.
  //
  // This was 1 — CSS pixels, which on any 2x display is half the screen's
  // linear resolution and a quarter of its pixels (measured at 1440x900 CSS
  // on a dpr-2 screen: a 1440x900 backing store against 2880x1800 of actual
  // device pixels). That was chosen when the heatmap read as nothing but
  // blur passes over a soft shape, where the extra pixels genuinely resolve
  // nothing. 1.5 is the deliberate middle: 0.75 of device linear, 2.25x the
  // fragment work of 1 and 56% that of a full 2. Raise to 2 only with a
  // frame-cost measurement to back it — this is a full-viewport shader
  // competing with Lenis and ScrollTrigger on the same main thread.
  const RENDER_SCALE = 1.5

  function maxPixelCount(container: HTMLElement) {
    const cssPixels = (container.clientWidth || 1) * (container.clientHeight || 1)
    return Math.max(1, Math.round(cssPixels * RENDER_SCALE * RENDER_SCALE))
  }

  // The still is frame 0 of this same shader, so anyone who asked for less
  // motion or less data gets exactly the image the canvas would open on —
  // minus the WebGL context, the texture fetch and the render loop. Read
  // once per page, like every other reduced-motion check on the site.
  function prefersStill() {
    if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) return true
    const connection = (navigator as Navigator & { connection?: { saveData?: boolean } }).connection
    return connection?.saveData === true
  }

  // ShaderMount pauses itself when its element leaves the viewport, but a
  // sticky element never does: the hero stays parked at top:0 while the rest
  // of the page scrolls over it, and IntersectionObserver reports it fully
  // intersecting all the way down. Left alone, the fragment shader ran on
  // every frame of the whole homepage scroll, under content that hides it.
  // Speed 0 halts the library's rAF loop outright, so covered means no GPU
  // work at all; the frame counter simply resumes where it stopped.
  function isCovered(bg: HTMLElement) {
    // Covered once the next section's top reaches the viewport top. The
    // hero box is 2H + R tall and pulled up by H (see StickyHero), so that
    // happens at its own height minus the background's.
    const hero = bg.parentElement
    return !!hero && window.scrollY >= hero.offsetHeight - bg.offsetHeight
  }

  // Held across client-side navigations so returning to the homepage reuses
  // the decoded texture instead of waiting on the disk cache again.
  let shapePromise: Promise<HTMLImageElement> | null = null

  function loadShape() {
    shapePromise ??= new Promise<HTMLImageElement>((resolve, reject) => {
      const image = new Image()
      // The `load` event, not image.decode(): browsers defer decoding for a
      // document that isn't visible, and the decode() promise then simply
      // never settles — open the homepage in a background tab and the shader
      // would stay unmounted even after you switched to it. `load` is enough
      // for a WebGL texture upload, and it fires either way.
      image.addEventListener('load', () => resolve(image), { once: true })
      image.addEventListener('error', () => reject(new Error('Hero shader texture failed to load')), {
        once: true,
      })
      image.src = PROCESSED_TEXTURE
    }).catch((error) => {
      // Don't cache a rejection: a one-off failure would otherwise keep the
      // shader off for the rest of the session, since every later mount
      // awaits this same promise.
      shapePromise = null
      throw error
    })
    return shapePromise
  }

  // The still and the canvas are the same image, so the fade only has to
  // cover decode/compile jitter — long enough not to pop, short enough that
  // the section is never visibly mid-transition. Both directions are used:
  // a lost WebGL context winds the pair back before retrying, so the section
  // falls back to the still rather than to the bare gradient underneath it.
  function showShader(container: HTMLElement) {
    container.classList.remove('opacity-0')
    document.getElementById('hero-still')?.classList.add('opacity-0')
  }

  function showStill(container: HTMLElement | null) {
    container?.classList.add('opacity-0')
    document.getElementById('hero-still')?.classList.remove('opacity-0')
  }

  let mount: ShaderMount | null = null
  let mobileQuery: MediaQueryList | null = null
  let resizeObserver: ResizeObserver | null = null
  let onQueryChange: (() => void) | null = null
  let onScroll: (() => void) | null = null
  let onContextLost: ((event: Event) => void) | null = null
  let onFrameKey: ((event: KeyboardEvent) => void) | null = null
  let contextRetried = false
  // Bumped by every teardown so an in-flight mount (loading the shape is
  // async) can tell it was superseded and drop its result instead of
  // attaching a canvas to a section that has already been swapped out.
  let generation = 0

  async function init() {
    const container = document.getElementById('hero-shader')
    // Only the homepage renders it; this script runs on every page that mounts it.
    if (!container || mount) return
    // Checked before the texture fetch so the gated visitor pays for nothing.
    if (prefersStill()) return
    const section = container.parentElement
    if (!section) return

    const token = generation
    const image = await loadShape()
    if (token !== generation || !container.isConnected) return

    mobileQuery = window.matchMedia(MOBILE_QUERY)

    mount = new ShaderMount(
      container,
      FRAGMENT_SHADER,
      {
        u_image: image,
        // Fill is set to a gradient in Paper (4A40F2 → 0E004D), and the shader
        // itself only takes a solid u_colorBack — so colorBack is transparent
        // here and the section's own CSS background gradient shows through
        // wherever the heatmap's opacity is less than 1.
        u_colorBack: [0, 0, 0, 0],
        u_colors: [
          getShaderColorFromString('#0E004D'),
          getShaderColorFromString('#4A40F2'),
          getShaderColorFromString('#2E0EB2'),
          getShaderColorFromString('#0E004D'),
        ],
        u_colorsCount: 4,
        u_angle: 270,
        u_noise: 0,
        u_innerGlow: 0.22,
        u_outerGlow: 0.29,
        u_contour: 0.309,
        u_fit: ShaderFitOptions.cover,
        u_rotation: 0,
        u_originX: 0.5,
        u_originY: 0.5,
        u_offsetY: 0,
        u_worldWidth: 0,
        u_worldHeight: 0,
        u_frameStrength: FRAME_STRENGTH,
        ...viewParams(image, container),
      },
      undefined,
      SPEED,
      0,
      // minPixelRatio: 1 so the library never *raises* the target above
      // device pixels; the ceiling below is what brings it down to CSS ones.
      1,
      maxPixelCount(container),
    )

    let covered = false
    onScroll = () => {
      const next = isCovered(section)
      if (next === covered) return
      covered = next
      mount?.setSpeed(covered ? 0 : SPEED)
    }

    onQueryChange = () => {
      mount?.setUniforms(viewParams(image, container))
      mount?.setMaxPixelCount(maxPixelCount(container))
      // A resize moves the section's bottom edge, which is the pause line.
      onScroll?.()
    }
    mobileQuery.addEventListener('change', onQueryChange)
    // Only load-bearing while the asset isn't square, but it's what keeps a
    // non-square one filling as the section's aspect ratio changes — window
    // drags, rotation, the mobile URL bar collapsing on scroll. setUniforms
    // skips unchanged values, so with a square asset this costs nothing.
    resizeObserver = new ResizeObserver(onQueryChange)
    resizeObserver.observe(container)

    // Lenis drives the native scroller, so window's own scroll event is the
    // right signal. Run once up front: a reload or bfcache restore can land
    // mid-page, with the hero already covered.
    window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true })
    onScroll()

    // Mobile Safari drops WebGL contexts under memory pressure (backgrounding
    // the tab is enough), which leaves the canvas blank and the section
    // showing only its CSS gradient. preventDefault marks the loss as
    // recoverable; one remount is enough, and the retry is one-shot so a
    // device that simply can't hold a context doesn't loop.
    onContextLost = (event) => {
      event.preventDefault()
      if (contextRetried) return
      contextRetried = true
      teardown()
      start()
    }
    mount.canvasElement.addEventListener('webglcontextlost', onContextLost)

    if (import.meta.env.DEV) {
      let frameOn = FRAME_STRENGTH > 0
      onFrameKey = (event) => {
        if (!event.shiftKey || event.code !== 'KeyF') return
        if ((event.target as HTMLElement | null)?.closest('input, textarea, [contenteditable]')) return
        frameOn = !frameOn
        mount?.setUniforms({ u_frameStrength: frameOn ? 1 : 0 })
        console.info(`[hero] virtual frame ${frameOn ? 'on' : 'off'}`)
      }
      window.addEventListener('keydown', onFrameKey)
    }

    // Cross-fades the canvas in as the still fades out. The canvas is fully
    // transparent for the first frame of the fade, which covers the gap
    // before ShaderMount's own rAF gets its first paint up.
    showShader(container)
  }

  function teardown() {
    generation += 1
    // Only observable on the context-loss retry, where the section stays on
    // screen — after a navigation the element is replaced wholesale anyway.
    showStill(mount?.parentElement ?? document.getElementById('hero-shader'))
    if (mobileQuery && onQueryChange) mobileQuery.removeEventListener('change', onQueryChange)
    if (onScroll) window.removeEventListener('scroll', onScroll)
    if (onFrameKey) window.removeEventListener('keydown', onFrameKey)
    resizeObserver?.disconnect()
    if (mount && onContextLost) mount.canvasElement.removeEventListener('webglcontextlost', onContextLost)
    // Frees the GL program/textures and removes the canvas — without it a
    // client-side navigation would leak a context per visit, and browsers
    // cap how many can be live at once.
    mount?.dispose()
    mount = null
    mobileQuery = null
    resizeObserver = null
    onQueryChange = null
    onScroll = null
    onContextLost = null
    onFrameKey = null
  }

  // Setup/teardown pair per feature (see docs/conventions.md): the pair is
  // what lets a back/forward-cache restore remount the shader instead of
  // coming back to a section that lost its WebGL context while frozen.
  // A failed mount is survivable — the section keeps its CSS gradient — so
  // the rejection is swallowed rather than left to surface as an unhandled
  // promise error in the console.
  function start() {
    void init().catch(() => {})
  }

  onPageLoad(() => {
    // A fresh navigation gets a fresh retry budget; the recovery path itself
    // doesn't, so a device that can't hold a context stops after one attempt.
    contextRetried = false
    start()
  })
  onPageUnload(teardown)
</script>
